#subtitle: Working hours, safety regulations, and unemployment relief.

= Labor Rights

With control of the Labor Ministry, we can set national standards for labor. Our supporters expect us to enforce the eight-hour day, extend safety regulations into the mines and factories, and build up whatever relief we can offer the growing ranks of the unemployed.

# enforce the eight-hour day
# develop and enforce safety regulations in mines and industries
# works councils are part of another card...

@labor_rights_menu

- @working_hours: Enforce the eight-hour day.
- @shorten_hours: Shorten work hours further, below eight a day.
- @safety: Develop and enforce mine and factory safety regulations.
- @unemployment_insurance: Fund unemployment relief.
- @root: Do not enact any policies.
- @easy_discard
- @cancel_advisor_action

@working_hours
view-if: working_hours < 2
on-arrival: working_hours += 1; labor_dissent -= 8; left_dissent -= 5; labor_strength += 5; industrial_psoe += 7*(1-dissent); radical_relation -= 4; izq_rep_relation -= 2; coalition_dissent += 1; capital_strike_progress += 1 if radical_relation <= 30; inflation += 0.1; economic_growth -= 0.1

The eight-hour day has been the law on paper since 1919, but enforcement has grown porous, especially in the countryside and in smaller workshops. Employers have found no shortage of exceptions and loopholes, returning many workers to the old regime of forced overtime.

Now, with the force of the Labor Ministry behind it, we are finally enforcing workers' right to their own time. Employers and their political representatives are displeased.

@shorten_hours
view-if: working_hours >= 2 and black_thursday_seen = 1
subtitle: Could we induce more hiring and reduce unemployment by shortening work hours further?
on-arrival: working_hours += 1; labor_dissent -= 8; left_dissent -= 5; labor_strength += 5; industrial_psoe += 7*(1-dissent); radical_relation -= 4; izq_rep_relation -= 3; coalition_dissent += 1; capital_strike_progress += 1; unemployed -= 0.3; economic_growth -= 0.2

Some in the labor movement argue that reducing the maximum hours worked would induce employers to hire more workers, reducing unemployment, without cutting take-home pay. Employers resist, arguing they need more flexibility in hiring and firing, not less.

@safety
on-arrival: workers_safety += 1; labor_dissent -= 5; labor_strength += 2; industrial_psoe += 5*(1-dissent); capital_strike_progress += 1 if workers_safety >= 3; radical_relation -= 1 if workers_safety >= 3; ceda_relation -= 1 if workers_safety >= 3

The Labor Ministry works to develop and enforce safety regulations in the Asturian coal pits and the factories of Catalonia and the Basque country, in order to secure healthier working conditions for industrial labor. [? if workers_safety >= 3 : Employers have begun to argue that our focus on safety is overbearing, interfering unduly with production. ?]

@unemployment_insurance
view-if: unemployed >= unemployment_insurance_threshold + 4
subtitle: With unemployment rising, we must find new ways to fund relief for the jobless.

Spain has never had anything like Germany's unemployment insurance system — what exists is a patchwork of municipal relief committees and public-works subsidies. With the onset of the Depression and its throngs of unemployed, that patchwork is under severe strain. Something must be done to balance the budget while still providing for the jobless.

- @cut_benefits: Cut relief spending.
- @increase_employers: Fund relief through a levy on employers.
- @balance: Increase both employer and general-budget contributions.
- @labor_rights_menu: We can defer this decision for now.

@cut_benefits
subtitle: The workers will regard this as a betrayal.
on-arrival: industrial_psoe -= 0.12*industrial_psoe; unemployed_psoe -= 0.2*unemployed_psoe; industrial_pce += 5; unemployed_pce += 5; left_dissent += 15; center_dissent += 10; labor_dissent += 15; reformist_dissent += 5; welfare -= 2; budget += 1; unemployed += 3; pro_republic -= 4; pce_relation -= 8; inflation -= 2; unemployment_insurance_threshold = unemployed; unemployment_insurance_timer = 10; economic_growth -= 0.2

For budgetary reasons, we have cut relief spending. Our reasons for fiscal discipline are alien to the workers, and they are furious at our betrayal, as are a good portion of our own membership across the party's factions. Our loss is the <span style="color: #700000;">**PCE**</span>'s gain.

@increase_employers
subtitle: Employers are strongly against this. [? if in_republican_socialist : The Radical Party will likely take this badly. ?]
on-arrival: capital_strike_progress += 2; coalition_dissent = 2 if in_republican_socialist; coalition_dissent += 1; welfare += 1; industrial_psoe += 6*(1-dissent); unemployed_psoe += 6*(1-dissent); smallholder_psoe -= 6*(1-dissent); urban_middle_psoe -= 2*(1-dissent); unemployment_insurance_threshold = unemployed; unemployment_insurance_timer = 10; radical_relation -= 6; ceda_relation -= 4; izq_rep_relation -= 4; economic_growth -= 0.2

We have levied employers to fund unemployment relief, allowing the program to continue without further squeezing the budget. The propertied classes and their political parties are furious, while our base among the unions and the unemployed are pleased.

@balance
subtitle: Neither workers nor employers will be entirely satisfied.
on-arrival: industrial_psoe -= 4; unemployed_psoe -= 5; industrial_pce += 3; unemployed_pce += 3; left_dissent += 10; labor_dissent += 8; center_dissent += 5; welfare -= 1; budget += 1; pce_relation -= 4; inflation -= 1; unemployment_insurance_threshold = unemployed; unemployment_insurance_timer = 10; coalition_dissent += 1 if in_republican_socialist; economic_growth -= 0.1

Neither side is entirely satisfied by our compromise. Many workers and unemployed see this as a betrayal, although we present it as the successful aversion of a much worse alternative.
